		{
			"type":"file",
			"file":"3 Concepts/Heat Capacity.md",
			"id":"8db6189a66c1580a",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1067,
			"y":204,
			"width":284,
			"height":215,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"f9d396c5ed20f3e1",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# The Purpose of Solid State Physics\nWhat is the purpose of Solid State Physics? I would summarize it as \"An attempt at understanding **macroscopic aspects of solids** that emerge from the clumping of their constituent elements\". That is to say that we wish to understand how our microscopic theories give rise to properties like heat capacity, thermal and electrical conductivities, optical properties and more. And hopefully in doing so, we might come across unexpected and exotic phenomena that are subject to current research.\n\nThis framing of the field puts it on an equal footing with statistical physics, and indeed solid state physics can be thought of as a subfield of statistical physics as it deals with macroscopic emergent behaviors - a lot of the times (and specifically in introductory courses) in the thermodynamic limit.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2320,
			"y":-1451,
			"width":840,
			"height":360
		},
		{
			"id":"fcfede73468208ee",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# The \"Usual\" Structure of a Solid State Introduction\nThe study of solid state physics often begins with metals, and specifically the dynamics of free electrons in a crystalline lattice. We first step makes the simplest assumptions and treats the electrons as classical particles that are subject to classical EM forces and collide with the ions of the lattice, giving rise to classical-stochastic dynamics which manages to capture a surprising amount of insight given that it is a very crude theory. This is known as the Drude theory of free electrons and it is usually accompanied with the Lorentz oscillator model for bound electrons, and together they provide a simple and neat framework for electric response and thermal behaviors in solid which is qualitatively correct for the most part.\n\nThe second part takes a a somewhat radical new approach and neglects the lattice altogether, but treats the electrons as matter waves which is more true to their nature. This is known as the Sommerfeld theory, and it manages to produce more accurate results for free electron gas which is to say for metals. ",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2320,
			"y":-991,
			"width":840,
			"height":521
		},
